      "content": "For recognizing non-verbal cues, have them observe the patient's body language, eye contact, and facial expressions. Teach them to look for tension or rigidity in the muscles, unusual gestures or postures, and avoidance of eye contact. They should also be aware of rapid breathing patterns, clenching or gritting of the teeth, or sudden changes in vocal tone. Some key phrases to describe these behaviors include \"You seem tense to me,\" \"I notice you're trying to pull away,\" or \"Can you tell me if you're feeling overwhelmed?\" to label the behavior without escalating the situation.",
